.TITLE WELCOME TO OS/MICRO
.
If you can see this, you have successfully booted into OS/M and run your first program - the pager.
.
.NEXTPARA
From here, you can try whatever you want from the shell. I would recommend trying the XO game, as primitive as it is, because it shows you what to expect from 'graphics' on the Ozpex Micro.
.
.NEXTPARA
In reality, there is not much to actually do from within OS/M. Only a few programs exist for it that I know of, and those programs mainly serve as demos of the system. The primary purpose of OS/M and the Ozpex Micro as a whole is to serve as a rewarding and simple retro system to target and practice your assembly skills in.
.
.NEXTPARA
To that end, this document will help you get started with OS/M development. There don't currently exist any development tools that run from within the OS/M environment, so you will have to cross-assemble. Thankfully, it is incredibly easy to start writing assembly for OS/M on your machine.
.
.NEXTPARA
First, you will need a Z80 assembler. I recommend vasmz80_oldstyle,
which you can find here:
.LINK http://www.compilers.de/vasm.html
Once you have the right vasm binary, you will also need the OS/M libraries which you can download from my Github releases page:
.LINK https://github.com/BeauConstrictor/OS-Micro/releases
That should be all you need (besides an Ozpex Micro emulator, but I assume you already have one if you are reading this). As a quick sanity test, you can compile this quick 'Hello, world!' program and build it with vasm:
.
.CODE
    .include "mmap.asm"
    .org RUN_LOAD
  start:
    ld   hl,message
    call print
    ret
  message:
    .asciiz "Hello, world!\\n"
    .include "io.asm"
.ENDCODE
.
This is how you assemble that program (assuming you named it \fI'hello.asm'\fR):
.
.CODE
vasmz80_oldstyle -Fbin -dotdir -esc -o hello hello.asm
.ENDCODE
.
Then, you need to create a disk image which you can load into your emulator. The OS/M release includes a utility for this called \fBfs128-tool.py\fR. Here is how you would pack that binary into an FS/128 image using it:
.
.CODE
python3 fs128-tool.py -o mydisk.bin hello
.ENDCODE
.
This will output a \fI'mydisk.bin'\fR file whihc you can load into your Ozpex Micro emulator. If you are using my emulator, \fBozm\fR, this is the command that you would use:
.
.CODE
ozm -m bdsk:osm.bin@0 disk:mydisk.bin@1
.ENDCODE
This command will mount the \fBosm\fR distribution into device slot 00 and your new disk into slot 01.
.
.NEXTPARA
From here, you can switch to disk 01 and run your prgoram! If you haven't used the \fBdsk\fR command before, here is what you would need to do:
.
.CODE
  00 ~> dsk 01
  01 ~> dir
  hello
  01 ~> hello
  Hello, world!
.ENDCODE
.
Congratulations! You have just built and run your first OS/M program.
.
.TITLE LIBRARIES
From here, you should start experimenting with the OS/M libraries.
Unlike most retro OSs such as CP/M, these libraries are actually
assembled along with your program and interact directly with the
hardware - they don't go through a BIOS. This is because the Ozpex
Micro's hardware model is simple and extensible enough that you can
implement it individually in every program on your computer without
bloating the binary sizes too much.
.
.NEXTPARA
If you want to read more about the Ozpex Micro's device model, check
back on the repo soon, as I am in the middle of writing a document
on that topic.
.
.NEXTPARA
Currently, 3 libraries are included, which you can find in the
release on Github:

1.\h'5n' fs.asm - The most complex library, implements the FS/128 
.in +3n
filesystem to allow you to read, write, create, etc. files. This filesystem was originally designed for the Ozpex 128, but that similar device model means it could be carried over.
.in -3n
.sp 0
2.\h'5n' io.asm -  The library you will use most often, includes
.in +3n
I/O routines for printing strings, reading lines of input and parsing and outputting hex.
.in -3n
.sp 0
3.\h'5n' dev.asm - A small library currently, that lets you pro-
.in +3n
gramatically discover devices installed in your machine. This one will be more useful in the future when the Ozpex Micro includes more standard devices.
.in -3n

Let's start with io.asm, as this is the only library that your 'Hello, world!' program from earlier includes. You have already seen the 'print' routine, which takes, in the hl register, a pointer to a sequence of characters followed by a terminating null, or 0, byte. Vasm allows you to create one of these 'strings' easily using the '.asciiz' directive.
.
.NEXTPARA
For a more thorough description of every subroutine included in every library, I implore you to read through the source code. which contains plenty of comments for documentation. I find it easier to keep my documentation up-to-date when it is in the same place as the source code.
.
.NEXTPARA
Otherwise, I hope you are now in a comfortable position to start writing Z80 assembly! If you still feel lost, take a look at any of the programs I have written that go into the distribution disk. I'm told they're quite readable, and I haven't spent much time on any one of them (xo took me a grand total of 10 minutes), so they're all fairly simple.
